by nicolas-grekas at 2026-09-16T16:46:16Z

Correction to my previous comment: the trigger degradation moved from `MessageContext` to `ScheduledStamp`, and it is now a `__serialize()` with no `__unserialize()` at all.

The version I described added `MessageContext::__unserialize()`, which assigned `$data['name']` and `$data['id']` into `string`-typed properties. PHP's own unserialize rejects an object there with a `TypeError`, but an assignment inside `__unserialize()` runs in coercive mode, so a `Stringable` in the payload fires `__toString()`: a new trampoline into a gadget chain. `UnserializeToStringTrampolineRule` did not catch it, because its guard check is method-wide and the unrelated `is_string($data['trigger'])` in the same method counted as the guard. All 13 checks were green on that commit.

`ScheduledStamp::__serialize()` returns the rebuilt context under the same `messageContext` key and defines no `__unserialize()`, so PHP restores the property natively, with its strict type check intact, and payloads written before 8.2 decode unchanged. No string-typed property is ever assigned from a payload.
